<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The parties married in November 2008 and lived in Jamshedpur; a daughter was born of the marriage. The husband&rsquo;s petition alleged quarrels, an incident in which a hot pan was thrown at family members, mistreatment of the child, and — centrally — that the wife left when the daughter was about seven months old and never returned, not even to accompany the child&rsquo;s cancer treatment in Mumbai. The Family Court, Jamshedpur granted divorce on cruelty and desertion in November 2021, with interim maintenance of Rs 10,000 per month. In the High Court, an amicus was appointed for the wife and settlement was attempted on permanent alimony, without success.</p>

<h2>The pleading rules do real work</h2>
<p>The Division Bench&rsquo;s first move was procedural. The wife&rsquo;s written statement answered the petition&rsquo;s detailed allegations with evasive, general denials. Under Order VIII Rule 4 CPC, an evasive denial is treated as no denial; under Rule 5, facts not specifically denied are deemed admitted. The cruelty and desertion allegations therefore stood admitted on the pleadings themselves.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;Order VIII Rule 5 of the CPC provides that facts not denied shall be deemed to be admitted.&rdquo;</p>
</div>

<h2>Independent findings beyond the admissions</h2>
<p>The court did not rest on the deemed admissions alone. It held, independently, that abandoning a seven-month-old infant, coupled with indifference to the child&rsquo;s cancer treatment, amounted to cruelty; that the wife&rsquo;s own statement in cross-examination established desertion; and that a criminal case under Section 498A IPC lodged only after the divorce petition, with no dowry cruelty pleaded in the matrimonial case, operated as a further element of cruelty. An allegation of psychiatric illness against the wife was found unsubstantiated and played no part in the outcome. The court concluded that the husband &ldquo;successfully proved that the appellant subjected the respondent to cruelty and deserted the respondent&rdquo;.</p>

<h2>Divorce affirmed, maintenance raised</h2>
<p>Having affirmed the decree, the Bench turned to the wife&rsquo;s financial protection and enhanced the maintenance from Rs 10,000 to Rs 15,000 per month, expressly modifiable on changed circumstances. The two holdings sit together comfortably: the marriage ended on proof of the statutory grounds, while the obligation of support continued and was recalibrated.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For respondents in divorce cases.</strong> The written statement is evidence architecture, not a formality. Meet every material allegation specifically; where an incident is denied, say what actually happened. Evasive drafting can concede the case before trial begins.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For petitioners.</strong> Plead with particularity. Deemed admissions operate only where the petition itself is specific enough that silence or evasion is meaningful.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For maintenance strategy.</strong> Losing the decree battle does not end the support question. Appellate courts can and do enhance maintenance while dismissing the appeal, and modification remains available on changed circumstances.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>Matrimonial pleadings are too often treated as ritual — a petition of standard adjectives met by a written statement of standard denials. This judgment, from a Bench headed by the Chief Justice, is a reminder that the CPC&rsquo;s admission machinery applies with full force in the Family Court, and that conduct towards a child — abandonment, indifference to serious illness — weighs heavily in the cruelty calculus. The post-petition 498A finding continues a line of authority treating retaliatory criminal process, unsupported by pleaded facts, as itself a species of cruelty, while the maintenance enhancement shows the court&rsquo;s equally firm hand on the protective side of the ledger.</p>
